1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,135
This is the last night of Troy.

2
00:00:02,435 --> 00:00:05,700
ILOS: They're gone! The Greeks are gone! Ten years, and they just sailed away!

3
00:00:05,950 --> 00:00:09,758
YOU: And they left us a giant wooden horse. Doesn't that seem strange to you?

4
00:00:10,008 --> 00:00:12,236
ILOS: It's a gift to the gods, brother. Drink! We won!

5
00:00:12,585 --> 00:00:21,492
You are a Trojan soldier. You survived ten years of war. In six hours, your city will burn. To see how, go back ten years.

6
00:00:23,692 --> 00:00:33,319
Troy. A rich city on the coast of Anatolia, guarding the sea road to the Black Sea. Today, its ruins lie in Turkey, near Çanakkale.

7
00:00:33,619 --> 00:00:37,682
FATHER: Hold the reins steady, son. Troy breeds the finest horses in the world.

8
00:00:37,932 --> 00:00:41,760
ILOS: Did you see her at the harbor? They say she's the most beautiful woman alive.

9
00:00:42,010 --> 00:00:44,863
YOU: I saw a lot of Spartan gold. And a lot of guards.

10
00:00:45,163 --> 00:00:49,358
YOU: Father, is it true? Prince Paris came back from Sparta with another man's wife?

11
00:00:49,608 --> 00:00:54,985
FATHER: Helen. Queen of Sparta. And her husband's brother is Agamemnon, the most powerful king in Greece.

12
00:00:55,235 --> 00:00:56,660
YOU: So what happens now?

13
00:00:56,910 --> 00:00:58,920
FATHER: Now? Now we sharpen our spears.

14
00:00:59,220 --> 00:01:00,799
YOU: A war? Over one woman?

15
00:01:01,049 --> 00:01:07,779
FATHER: Wars are never about one woman, son. Every ship that sails to the Black Sea passes our coast. The Greeks want what we have.

16
00:01:09,979 --> 00:01:12,166
A year later, you're on the wall at dawn.

17
00:01:12,416 --> 00:01:14,538
ILOS: Gods. Look at the sea. How many ships are there?

18
00:01:14,788 --> 00:01:16,756
YOU: I stopped counting at three hundred.

19
00:01:17,056 --> 00:01:19,260
ILOS: Look! Prince Hector is coming down from the palace!

20
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:27,722
Homer lists more than a thousand ships. Probably far fewer. But to you, it looks like the whole world has come for Troy.

21
00:01:28,022 --> 00:01:30,759
HECTOR: Trojans! To the beach! Push them back into the sea!

22
00:01:31,059 --> 00:01:38,299
A prophecy said the first Greek to step onto Trojan soil would die. He jumps anyway. He dies at once.

23
00:01:38,549 --> 00:01:39,737
ILOS: Ha! Who wants to be second?

24
00:01:40,037 --> 00:01:42,621
Thousands do. The Greeks take the beach.

25
00:01:44,821 --> 00:01:50,362
But they can't take the city. So they build a camp on the shore, and they stay. Year after year.

26
00:01:50,662 --> 00:01:53,550
YOU: Nine years, Ilos. I was sixteen when this started.

27
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:55,851
ILOS: And now you're old and ugly. Pass the bread.

28
00:01:56,101 --> 00:01:59,740
ILOS: My uncle says the Greeks are so hungry, they're eating their own horses.

29
00:01:59,990 --> 00:02:02,528
YOU: Your uncle also says he once wrestled a lion.

30
00:02:02,778 --> 00:02:04,300
ILOS: A small lion. It still counts.

31
00:02:04,650 --> 00:02:06,869
YOU: Father, why don't they just attack the walls?

32
00:02:07,119 --> 00:02:10,919
FATHER: Look at them. The old songs say Poseidon himself built these walls.

33
00:02:11,169 --> 00:02:11,841
YOU: Did he?

34
00:02:12,041 --> 00:02:15,881
FATHER: No. But the walls are real. And no ladder in Greece is tall enough.

35
00:02:16,181 --> 00:02:24,556
A full bronze armor costs as much as a farm. Most men fight with a spear, a shield of wood and leather, and a lot of hope.

36
00:02:24,856 --> 00:02:30,812
FATHER: Shield high. And never, ever break the line. A line of shields is a wall. A man alone is just a target.

37
00:02:31,062 --> 00:02:32,910
YOU: Yes, Father. Never break the line.

38
00:02:35,110 --> 00:02:38,872
SCOUT: News from the Greek camp! Achilles has quarreled with King Agamemnon!

39
00:02:39,122 --> 00:02:40,499
ILOS: Achilles? Their best fighter?

40
00:02:40,699 --> 00:02:44,316
SCOUT: He's sitting in his tent. He swears he won't fight for them anymore.

41
00:02:44,566 --> 00:02:45,962
YOU: What did they fight about?

42
00:02:46,162 --> 00:02:51,716
SCOUT: A captive girl named Briseis. Agamemnon took her from him, so Achilles took himself out of the war.

43
00:02:52,016 --> 00:02:54,480
HECTOR: Then tomorrow we attack. We will burn their ships!

44
00:02:54,730 --> 00:02:57,069
YOU: If their ships burn, they can never go home.

45
00:02:57,319 --> 00:03:00,291
ILOS: Then they'll fight like wolves in a corner. Sleep well, brother.

46
00:03:00,591 --> 00:03:08,480
This is what Homer's Iliad is really about: the anger of Achilles. And for the first time in nine years, Troy is winning.

47
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:16,467
Dawn. The gates open, and thousands of Trojans pour onto the plain. You're in the front line.

48
00:03:16,717 --> 00:03:22,074
HECTOR: Trojans! Today we fight for our wives, our children, and the walls of our fathers! Forward!

49
00:03:22,374 --> 00:03:25,212
ILOS: Shields up! Stay together! Whatever happens, stay next to me!

50
00:03:25,462 --> 00:03:30,962
Chariots thunder past. Arrows hiss overhead. Then the two armies crash together.

51
00:03:31,212 --> 00:03:32,202
ILOS: Chariot! Get down!

52
00:03:32,402 --> 00:03:33,808
YOU: Ilos! On your left!

53
00:03:34,008 --> 00:03:35,208
ILOS: I see him! Push! Push!

54
00:03:35,408 --> 00:03:37,268
YOU: Hold the line! Don't break the line!

55
00:03:37,518 --> 00:03:39,073
HECTOR: Fire! Bring fire to the ships!

56
00:03:39,373 --> 00:03:47,053
The first Greek ship goes up in flames. And then, out of the smoke, a warrior in shining armor charges straight at you.

57
00:03:47,303 --> 00:03:48,890
YOU: Achilles! It's Achilles!

58
00:03:49,190 --> 00:03:57,856
It isn't. It's his best friend, Patroclus, wearing his armor. Hector cuts him down. And now, Achilles will come for revenge.

59
00:03:58,156 --> 00:03:59,472
YOU: Ilos! Are you hurt?

60
00:03:59,672 --> 00:04:01,774
ILOS: Just a scratch. Bronze is cheaper than skin.

61
00:04:03,974 --> 00:04:08,627
That evening, you're guarding the gate when Hector stops to say goodbye to his wife.

62
00:04:08,877 --> 00:04:12,819
ANDROMACHE: Hector, please. Stay inside the walls. If you die, what happens to us?

63
00:04:13,069 --> 00:04:17,258
HECTOR: If I hide, I could never look a Trojan in the eye again. I have to fight.

64
00:04:17,508 --> 00:04:18,844
ANDROMACHE: Then come back to us.

65
00:04:19,094 --> 00:04:21,797
HECTOR: I will try. That is all a soldier can promise.

66
00:04:22,097 --> 00:04:31,260
He reaches for his baby son. The child screams, terrified of the horsehair crest on his helmet. Hector laughs, takes it off, and kisses him.

67
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:35,173
HECTOR: One day, may they say of you: he is even braver than his father.

68
00:04:35,523 --> 00:04:40,932
The next day, Achilles returns. Every Trojan runs for the gates. Except one.

69
00:04:41,182 --> 00:04:42,506
ILOS: Hector! Get inside! Hector!

70
00:04:42,756 --> 00:04:44,535
ACHILLES: Hector! Turn and face me!

71
00:04:44,835 --> 00:04:49,043
Achilles chases him around the walls three times. Then it's over.

72
00:04:49,343 --> 00:04:51,553
YOU: Hector is dead. Who will protect Troy now?

73
00:04:51,903 --> 00:04:57,745
That night, old King Priam walks alone into the enemy camp, and kneels before Achilles.

74
00:04:57,995 --> 00:05:04,649
PRIAM: Remember your own father, Achilles. I have done what no man has done. I have kissed the hands of the man who killed my son.

75
00:05:04,949 --> 00:05:06,990
ACHILLES: Get up, old man. Take him home.

76
00:05:07,340 --> 00:05:12,187
The Iliad ends right here, with Hector's funeral. Not with the fall of Troy.

77
00:05:14,387 --> 00:05:21,605
Months later, you wake up to silence. The Greek camp is empty. And on the beach stands a giant wooden horse.

78
00:05:21,855 --> 00:05:24,752
ILOS: They've given up! It's an offering. We should take it to the temple!

79
00:05:25,002 --> 00:05:29,287
LAOCOÖN: Are you mad? Burn it! I fear the Greeks, even when they bring gifts!

80
00:05:29,537 --> 00:05:38,308
SINON: Wait! I am Sinon. The Greeks left me behind. The horse is an offering to Athena. They built it huge, so you could never take it into your city, because if you did, Troy could never fall.

81
00:05:38,558 --> 00:05:41,048
CASSANDRA: Fire! I see fire and blood! Troy is burning!

82
00:05:41,298 --> 00:05:46,836
YOU: That's Princess Cassandra. The gods cursed her. She always tells the truth, and nobody ever believes her.

83
00:05:47,086 --> 00:05:48,389
ILOS: She says that about everything.

84
00:05:48,639 --> 00:05:49,943
ILOS: Come on, grab a rope! Pull!

85
00:05:50,243 --> 00:05:55,889
The horse is too big for the gate. So the Trojans break open their own wall to bring it in.

86
00:05:56,189 --> 00:05:58,296
YOU: We just broke our own wall, for a horse.

87
00:05:58,546 --> 00:05:59,904
ILOS: Relax! Tonight, we celebrate!

88
00:06:02,104 --> 00:06:07,827
Back to tonight. Midnight. While Troy sleeps, a hidden door opens in the belly of the horse.

89
00:06:08,127 --> 00:06:10,319
YOU: Ilos. Ilos, wake up! Do you hear that?

90
00:06:10,569 --> 00:06:12,134
ILOS: The gates! Someone has opened the gates!

91
00:06:12,384 --> 00:06:14,165
YOU: Get your shield! Where's your spear?

92
00:06:14,365 --> 00:06:16,137
ILOS: Go! Find my mother! I'll hold them here!

93
00:06:16,437 --> 00:06:23,002
The Greek fleet was hiding behind the island of Tenedos. Now the whole army is inside the walls.

94
00:06:23,302 --> 00:06:26,425
AENEAS: Trojan! This way! The city is lost. Save who you can!

95
00:06:26,675 --> 00:06:28,338
YOU: Ilos! Ilos, come on!

96
00:06:28,588 --> 00:06:29,756
ILOS: Go! Remember me, brother!

97
00:06:30,056 --> 00:06:35,040
You follow Prince Aeneas through the smoke. He carries his old father on his back.

98
00:06:35,290 --> 00:06:36,543
YOU: Where are we going?

99
00:06:36,743 --> 00:06:40,017
AENEAS: Wherever the sea takes us. As long as we live, Troy lives.

100
00:06:40,367 --> 00:06:45,616
Roman legend says Aeneas sailed west to Italy, and his descendants founded Rome.

101
00:06:47,816 --> 00:06:49,298
So, was any of it real?

102
00:06:49,598 --> 00:07:00,359
In eighteen seventy-one, Heinrich Schliemann dug into a hill called Hisarlık, near Çanakkale. He found not one city, but nine, built on top of each other.

103
00:07:00,609 --> 00:07:02,392
YOU: Nine Troys? Which one was mine?

104
00:07:02,692 --> 00:07:11,821
One of them burned around eleven eighty BC. Archaeologists found signs of fire, and arrowheads and sling stones in its streets.

105
00:07:12,121 --> 00:07:23,550
And in Hittite archives, there are letters about a kingdom called Wilusa, which sounds a lot like Ilios, Homer's name for Troy. One of its kings was called Alaksandu.

106
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:26,753
YOU: Alaksandu. Alexandros. That's Paris's other name!

107
00:07:27,053 --> 00:07:28,022
YOU: And the horse?

108
00:07:28,272 --> 00:07:39,519
The horse isn't in the Iliad at all. Some historians think it's a memory of a siege engine. Or of an earthquake: Poseidon was the god of both horses and earthquakes.

109
00:07:39,819 --> 00:07:41,468
YOU: So the war might have been real?

110
00:07:41,718 --> 00:07:47,086
The city was real. And it burned. The rest, historians are still arguing about.

111
00:07:47,436 --> 00:07:54,188
So, if you'd been standing in that square, looking up at the horse, would you have pulled it inside? Tell me in the comments.
